Glad to find this issue, as I was doing tons of tests about web security patch I faced problems to shutdown Kodi. I never waited 5 mins, but with the DEMO PVR addon on Windows i7 hexa core / 64Gb ram / SSD stopping Kodi always block on this for more than 1 / 2 minutes after then I give up and kills it :)

Would gladly test a Windows x64 build if there was a fix for that.
@basilgello open feature requests, but PVR is the poor child of JSON API not sure things are properly exposed.

> To be honest, I'm astonished that the patch actually works - especially that time is reduced that drastically. On my test system, I was not able to measure any difference. Before and after the patch, storing 180 channels with 30 days of EPG took 5 seconds (macOS + i7 + SSD).
> 
> So, I really would like to get feedback from some more users.

@ksooo Your feeling was right. While testing on a HDD I can trigger the long shutdown time by changing the VDR server in VNSI client and exit kodi. In this [debug log](http://ix.io/2rq2) it takes 1:58 minutes for ~150 channels.

Tried to implement the advice "Use a Transaction" on channel base:
```diff
diff --git a/xbmc/pvr/epg/EpgTagsContainer.cpp b/xbmc/pvr/epg/EpgTagsContainer.cpp
index 9cd5a36222..f057b5ed0b 100644
--- a/xbmc/pvr/epg/EpgTagsContainer.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/pvr/epg/EpgTagsContainer.cpp
@@ -581,6 +581,7 @@ void CPVREpgTagsContainer::Persist(bool bCommit)
     CLog::Log(LOGDEBUG, "EPG Tags Container: Updating %d, deleting %d events...",
               m_changedTags.size(), m_deletedTags.size());
 
+    m_database->BeginMultipleExecute();
     for (const auto& tag : m_deletedTags)
       m_database->Delete(*tag.second);
 
@@ -599,6 +600,7 @@ void CPVREpgTagsContainer::Persist(bool bCommit)
 
     if (bCommit)
       m_database->CommitInsertQueries();
+    m_database->CommitMultipleExecute();
 
     m_database->Unlock();
   }
```
Now the persisting time is reduced to i.e. [< 3 seconds](http://ix.io/2rq5).

Can this break anything?

> Has the patch been PR’d?

No. There are issues with it, still WIP.

@emveepee Can you try [this patch](http://ix.io/2sCC)? It is about eight times faster on the HDD, I'm wondering if there are improvements too on the NVMe

@phunkyfish Cite from the [kodi wiki](https://kodi.wiki/view/Advancedsettings.xml#musicdatabase.2Fvideodatabase):

> Kodi also supports using MySQL for PVR (tvdatabase and epgdatabase) as well as the ADSP database. However, there is little to no practical benefit to using MySQL for those databases, and there has been very little testing with them (expect bugs!). Unless you simply want to experiment, or have a very specific need, you should avoid using MySQL for PVR and ADSP.

Is this still true?

@ksooo 
> My experience while experimenting with BeginMultipleExecute() was that it does not help to gain performance as it internally uses transactions (iirc).

Yes, starting with BeginMultipleExecute() the queries are queued and committed in one transaction by calling CommitMultipleExecute(). IMO together with locking the database exactly what we need.

